{"parentUuid":"ca1169e3-43e5-4f35-a72f-f53f05e9daf6","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"eed3b582-9d5c-4660-93a0-d07811f6212c","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"rustling-honking-llama","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01De1ZsuhVgpyRUcNZ23Tjag","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (47.4KB). Full output saved to: /logs/agent/sessions/projects/-root/eed3b582-9d5c-4660-93a0-d07811f6212c/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01De1ZsuhVgpyRUcNZ23Tjag.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n1:SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization\n2-with a Reference-Free Reward\n3-Yu Meng1∗ Mengzhou Xia2 ∗ Danqi Chen2\n4-Computer Science Department, University of Virginia\n--\n14-Abstract\n15-Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) is a widely used offline preference optimization algorithm that reparameterizes reward functions in reinforcement learning\n16-from human feedback (RLHF) to enhance simplicity and training stability. In this\n17:work, we propose SimPO, a simpler yet more effective approach. The effectiveness\n18:of SimPO is attributed to a key design: using the average log probability of a\n19-sequence as the implicit reward. This reward formulation better aligns with model\n20-generation and eliminates the need for a reference model, making it more compute\n21-and memory efficient. Additionally, we introduce a target reward margin to the\n22:Bradley-Terry objective to encourage a larger margin between the winning and\n23-losing responses, further improving the algorithm’s performance. We compare\n24:SimPO to DPO and its recent variants across various state-of-the-art training setups,\n25-including both base and instruction-tuned models such as Mistral, Llama 3, and\n26-Gemma 2. We evaluate on extensive chat-based evaluation benchmarks, including\n27:AlpacaEval 2, MT-Bench, and Arena-Hard. Our results demonstrate that SimPO\n28-consistently and significantly outperforms existing approaches without substantially\n29:increasing response length. Specifically, SimPO outperforms DPO by up to 6.4\n30-points on AlpacaEval 2 and by up to 7.5 points on Arena-Hard. Our top-performing\n31-model, built on Gemma-2-9B-it, achieves a 72.4% length-controlled win rate on\n32-AlpacaEval 2, a 59.1% win rate on Arena-Hard, and ranks 1st on Chatbot Arena\n--\n52-1\n53-\n54-Equal Contribution.\n55:Code and models can be found at https://github.com/princeton-nlp/SimPO.\n56-\n57-38th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024).\n58-\n--\n66-+4.4\n67-\n68-DPO\n69:SimPO\n70-\n71-+5.3\n72-\n--\n92-\n...\n</persisted-output>"}]},"uuid":"dede81ea-159d-43aa-bde3-b29f4254b8d9","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:15:58.679Z","toolUseResult":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"1:SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization\n2-with a Reference-Free Reward\n3-Yu Meng1∗ Mengzhou Xia2 ∗ Danqi Chen2\n4-Computer Science Department, University of Virginia\n--\n14-Abstract\n15-Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) is a widely used offline preference optimization algorithm that reparameterizes reward functions in reinforcement learning\n16-from human feedback (RLHF) to enhance simplicity and training stability. In this\n17:work, we propose SimPO, a simpler yet more effective approach. The effectiveness\n18:of SimPO is attributed to a key design: using the average log probability of a\n19-sequence as the implicit reward. This reward formulation better aligns with model\n20-generation and eliminates the need for a reference model, making it more compute\n21-and memory efficient. Additionally, we introduce a target reward margin to the\n22:Bradley-Terry objective to encourage a larger margin between the winning and\n23-losing responses, further improving the algorithm’s performance. We compare\n24:SimPO to DPO and its recent variants across various state-of-the-art training setups,\n25-including both base and instruction-tuned models such as Mistral, Llama 3, and\n26-Gemma 2. We evaluate on extensive chat-based evaluation benchmarks, including\n27:AlpacaEval 2, MT-Bench, and Arena-Hard. Our results demonstrate that SimPO\n28-consistently and significantly outperforms existing approaches without substantially\n29:increasing response length. Specifically, SimPO outperforms DPO by up to 6.4\n30-points on AlpacaEval 2 and by up to 7.5 points on Arena-Hard. Our top-performing\n31-model, built on Gemma-2-9B-it, achieves a 72.4% length-controlled win rate on\n32-AlpacaEval 2, a 59.1% win rate on Arena-Hard, and ranks 1st on Chatbot Arena\n--\n52-1\n53-\n54-Equal Contribution.\n55:Code and models can be found at https://github.com/princeton-nlp/SimPO.\n56-\n57-38th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024).\n58-\n--\n66-+4.4\n67-\n68-DPO\n69:SimPO\n70-\n71-+5.3\n72-\n--\n92-\n93-Arena-Hard Win Rate (%)\n94-DPO\n95:SimPO\n96-\n97-+1.2\n98-+4.5\n--\n111-Llama3 \n112-Base 8B Instruct 8B\n113-\n114:Figure 1: SimPO and DPO mainly differ in their reward formulation, as indicated in the shaded box.\n115:SimPO outperforms DPO significantly across a range of settings on AlpacaEval 2 and Arena-Hard.\n116-the metric used to guide generation, which is approximately the average log likelihood of a response\n117-generated by the policy model. We hypothesize that this discrepancy between training and inference\n118-may lead to suboptimal performance.\n119:In this work, we propose SimPO, a simple\n120-yet effective offline preference optimization algorithm (Figure 1). The core of\n121-our algorithm aligns the reward function\n122:in the preference optimization objective\n123:with the generation metric. SimPO consists\n124-of two major components: (1) a lengthnormalized reward, calculated as the average log probability of all tokens in a response using the policy model, and (2) a\n125-target reward margin to ensure the reward\n126-difference between winning and losing responses exceeds this margin. In summary,\n127:SimPO has the following properties:\n128-\n129-Table 1: Length-controlled (LC) and raw win rate (WR),\n130-and generation lengths of top models on the AlpacaEval\n--\n133-\n134-LC (%) WR (%) Len.\n135-\n136:Gemma-2-9B-it-SimPO\n137-72.4\n138-GPT-4 Turbo (04/09)\n139-55.0\n140-Gemma-2-9B-it\n141-51.1\n142:Llama-3-8B-Instruct-SimPO 44.7\n143-Claude 3 Opus\n144-40.5\n145-Llama-3-8B-Instruct-DPO\n--\n167-1919\n168-1899\n169-\n170:• Simplicity: SimPO does not require a reference model, making it more lightweight and easier to\n171-implement compared to DPO and other reference-based methods.\n172:• Significant performance advantage: Despite its simplicity, SimPO significantly outperforms\n173:DPO and its latest variants (e.g., a recent reference-free objective ORPO [42]). The performance\n174-advantage is consistent across various training setups and extensive chat-based evaluations, including AlpacaEval 2 [55, 28] and the challenging Arena-Hard [54] benchmark. It achieves up to a\n175-6.4 point improvement on AlpacaEval 2 and a 7.5 point improvement on Arena-Hard compared to\n176-DPO (Figure 1).\n177:• Minimal length exploitation: SimPO does not significantly increase response length compared to\n178-the SFT or DPO models (Table 1), indicating minimal length exploitation [28, 71, 85].\n179:Extensive analysis shows that SimPO utilizes preference data more effectively, leading to a more\n180-accurate likelihood ranking of winning and losing responses on a held-out validation set, which in\n181:turn translates to a better policy model. As shown in Table 1, our Gemma-2-9B-it-SimPO model\n182-achieves state-of-the-art performance, with a 72.4% length-controlled win rate on AlpacaEval 2\n183-and a 59.1% win rate on Arena-Hard, establishing it as the strongest open-source model under 10B\n184-parameters. Most notably, when evaluated on Chatbot Arena [17] with real user votes, our model\n--\n187-\n188-2\n189-\n190:SimPO: Simple Preference Optimization\n191-\n192-In this section, we first introduce the background of DPO (§2.1). Then we identify the discrepancy\n193-between DPO’s reward and the likelihood metric used for generation, and propose an alternative\n194:reference-free reward formulation that mitigates this issue (§2.2). Finally, we derive the SimPO\n195:objective by incorporating a target reward margin term into the Bradley-Terry model (§2.3).\n196-2\n197-\n198-As of September 16th, 2024.\n--\n212-(1)\n213-πref (y | x)\n214-where πθ is the policy model, πref is the reference policy, typically the supervised fine-tuned (SFT)\n215:model, and Z(x) is the partition function. By incorporating this reward formulation into the BradleyTerry (BT) ranking objective [11], p(yw ≻ yl | x) = σ (r(x, yw ) − r(x, yl )), DPO expresses the\n216-probability of preference data with the policy model rather than the reward model, yielding the\n217:following objective:\n218-\u0014\n219-\u0012\n220-\u0013\u0015\n--\n264-within language models [12, 41, 62]. Naturally, we consider replacing the reward formulation in DPO\n265-with pθ in Eq. (3), so that it aligns with the likelihood metric that guides generation. This results in a\n266-length-normalized reward:\n267:rSimPO (x, y) =\n268-\n269-|y|\n270-β X\n--\n283-enhancing memory and computational efficiency compared to reference-dependent algorithms.\n284-2.3\n285-\n286:The SimPO Objective\n287-\n288-Target reward margin. Additionally, we introduce a target reward margin term, γ > 0, to the\n289:Bradley-Terry objective to ensure that the reward for the winning response, r(x, yw ), exceeds the\n290-3\n291-\n292-\freward for the losing response, r(x, yl ), by at least γ:\n--\n299-margin typically improves generalization. In preference optimization, the two classes are the winning\n300-and losing responses for a single input. In practice, we observe that generation quality initially\n301-improves with an increasing target margin but degrades when the margin becomes too large (§4.3).\n302:One of DPO’s variants, IPO [6], als