 to GPT-4.\n570-7\n571-Many recent studies [90, 74] have extensively compared DPO and PPO [70]. We will leave the comparison\n572:of PPO and SimPO to future work.\n573-\n574-5\n575-\n--\n718-7.7\n719-7.5\n720-\n721:SimPO\n722-\n723-21.5\n724-\n--\n882-8.0\n883-8.0\n884-\n885:SimPO\n886-\n887-22.0\n888-\n--\n905-8.0\n906-\n907-ORPO [42]8 introduces a reference-model-free odd ratio term to directly contrast winning and losing\n908:responses with the policy model and jointly trains with the SFT objective. R-DPO [64] is a modified\n909-version of DPO that includes an additional regularization term to prevent exploitation of length. We\n910-thoroughly tune the hyperparameters for each baseline and report the best performance. We find that\n911-many variants of DPO do not empirically present an advantage over standard DPO. Further details\n--\n916-Experimental Results\n917-\n918-In this section, we present main results of our experiments, highlighting the superior performance of\n919:SimPO on various benchmarks and ablation studies (§4.1). We provide an in-depth understanding\n920-of the following components: (1) length normalization (§4.2), (2) the margin term γ (§4.3), and (3)\n921:why SimPO outperforms DPO (§4.4). Unless otherwise specified, the ablation studies are conducted\n922-using the Mistral-Base setting.\n923-4.1\n924-\n925-Main Results and Ablations\n926-\n927:SimPO consistently and significantly outperforms existing preference optimization methods.\n928-As shown in Table 4, while all preference optimization algorithms enhance performance over the SFT\n929:model, SimPO, despite its simplicity, achieves the best overall performance across all benchmarks and\n930-settings. These consistent and significant improvements highlight the robustness and effectiveness of\n931:SimPO. Notably, SimPO outperforms the best baseline by 3.6 to 4.8 points on the AlpacaEval 2 LC\n932:win rate across various settings. On Arena-Hard, SimPO consistently achieves superior performance,\n933-8\n934-\n935-ORPO can directly train on preference data without the SFT stage. For fair comparisons, we start ORPO\n--\n938-6\n939-\n940-\fTable 5: Ablation studies under Mistral-Base and Mistral-Instruct settings. We ablate each key design\n941:of SimPO: (1) removing length normalization in Eq. (4) (i.e., w/o LN); (2) setting target reward\n942-margin γ to be 0 in Eq. (6) (i.e., γ = 0).\n943-Mistral-Base (7B) Setting\n944-AlpacaEval 2 Arena-Hard\n--\n955-\n956-5.9\n957-\n958:SimPO\n959-\n960-21.5\n961-\n--\n982-10\n983-0\n984-\n985:πSimPO\n986:πSimPO w/o LN\n987-\n988-−10\n989-\n--\n1081-\n1082-1000\n1083-\n1084:(b) SimPO.\n1085-\n1086-0\n1087-−5\n--\n1100-\n1101-1000\n1102-\n1103:(c) SimPO without LN.\n1104-\n1105-Figure 2: Effect of length normalization (LN). (a) Relationship between reward margin and length\n1106-difference between winning and losing responses. (b) Spearman correlation between average log\n1107:probability and response length for SimPO. (c) Spearman correlation for SimPO without LN.\n1108-though it is occasionally surpassed by CPO [88]. We find that CPO generates responses that are, on\n1109:average, 50% longer than those generated by SimPO (See Table 10). Arena-Hard might favor longer\n1110-generations due to the absence of a length penalty in its evaluation.\n1111-Benchmark quality varies. Although all three benchmarks are widely adopted, we find that MTBench exhibits poor separability across different methods. Minor differences between methods on\n1112-MT-Bench may be attributed to randomness, likely due to the limited scale of its evaluation data and\n--\n1118-observe that the Instruct setting consistently outperforms the Base setting. This improvement is likely\n1119-due to the higher quality of SFT models used for initialization and the generation of more high-quality\n1120-preference data by these models.\n1121:Both key designs in SimPO are crucial. In Table 5, we demonstrate results from ablating each\n1122:key design of SimPO: (1) removing length normalization in Eq. (4) (i.e., w/o LN); (2) setting the\n1123-target reward margin to be 0 in Eq. (6) (i.e., γ = 0). Removing the length normalization has the most\n1124-negative impact on the results. Our examination reveals that this leads to the generation of long and\n1125-repetitive patterns, substantially degrading the overall quality of the output (See Appendix E). Setting\n1126:γ to 0 yields also leads to a performance degradation compared to SimPO, indicating that it is not the\n1127-optimal target reward margin. In the following subsections, we conduct in-depth analyses to better\n1128-understand both design choices.\n1129-4.2\n--\n1131-Length Normalization (LN) Prevents Length Exploitation\n1132-\n1133-LN leads to an increase in the reward difference for all preference pairs, regardless of their\n1134:length. The Bradley-Terry objective in Eq. (5) essentially aims to optimize the reward difference\n1135-9\n1136-Although our models excel on benchmarks, these evaluations have limitations, including restricted query\n1137-space and potential biases from model-based evaluations. Efforts like WildBench [93] aim to expand these\n1138:spaces, where SimPO models demonstrate competitive performance.\n1139-\n1140-7\n1141-\n--\n1218-on chosen responses under different γ values.\n1219-∆r = r(x, yw ) − r(x, yl ) to exceed the target margin γ. We investigate the relationship between\n1220-the learned reward differences and the length difference ∆l = |yw | − |yl | between the winning and\n1221:losing responses from the training set of UltraFeedback. We measure the difference of reward (rSimPO ;\n1222:Eq. (4)) using the SFT model, the SimPO model, and a model trained with SimPO but without length\n1223:normalization. We present the results in Figure 2a and observe that SimPO with LN consistently\n1224-achieves a positive reward margin for all response pairs, regardless of their length difference, and\n1225:consistently improves the margin over the SFT