) = β log ππrefθ (y|x)\n--\n1319-pw < p l\n1320-Generation metric\n1321-\n1322:SimPO\n1323-\n1324-0.8\n1325-\n--\n1337-0.2\n1338-\n1339-DPO (β = 0.1)\n1340:SimPO (β = 2)\n1341:SimPO (β = 10)\n1342-\n1343-0.0\n1344-\n--\n1360-\n1361-0\n1362-\n1363:SimPO\n1364-\n1365-100\n1366-\n--\n1374-\n1375-KL divergence on yw\n1376-\n1377:Figure 4: Comparison between SimPO and DPO, measured on UltraFeedback. (a) Spearman\n1378-correlation between average log probability and response length for DPO. (b) Contingency table of\n1379-rankings based on DPO rewards and the average log likelihood (measured on the training set). (c)\n1380:Reward accuracy of DPO and SimPO.\n1381-\n1382-β = 0.1 β = 0.01\n1383-\n--\n1387-\n1388-β=2\n1389-\n1390:SimPO\n1391-\n1392-(b) Performance w/ different β.\n1393-\n--\n1406-25\n1407-0\n1408-\n1409:(c) Efficiency of DPO vs. SimPO.\n1410-\n1411:Figure 5: Comparison between SimPO and DPO (continued). (a) With different β in DPO and\n1412:SimPO, KL divergence from the policy model to the reference model on yw . (b) AlpacaEval2 LC\n1413:win rate of DPO and SimPO with different β. (c) Runtime and memory usage for DPO and SimPO.\n1414-implicitly counteract length bias. As shown in Table 6 and Figure 4a, employing DPO reduces the\n1415-Spearman correlation coefficient between average log likelihood and response length compared to\n1416:the approach without any length normalization (referred to as “SimPO w/o LN”). However, it still\n1417:exhibits a stronger positive correlation when compared to SimPO.10\n1418-DPO reward mismatches generation likelihood. There Table 6: Spearman correlation ρ beis a divergence between DPO’s reward formulation, tween average log likelihood of different models and response length on a\n1419-rθ (x, y) = β log ππrefθ (y|x)\n1420-(y|x) , and the average log likelihood\n1421-1\n1422-metric, pθ (y | x) = |y| log πθ (y | x), which directly held-out set.\n1423-impacts generation. As shown in Figure 4b, among\n1424:SimPO w/o LN DPO SimPO\n1425-the instances on the UltraFeedback training set where\n1426-ρ\n1427-0.82\n1428-0.59\n1429-0.34\n1430-rθ (x, yw ) > rθ (x, yl ) , almost half of the pairs have\n1431:pθ (yw | x) < pθ (yl | x). In contrast, SimPO directly\n1432-employs the average log likelihood (scaled by β) as the reward expression, thereby eliminating the\n1433-discrepancy completely, as demonstrated in Figure 6b.\n1434:DPO lags behind SimPO in terms of reward accuracy. In Figure 4c, we compare the reward\n1435:accuracy of SimPO and DPO, assessing how well their final learned rewards align with preference\n1436:labels on a held-out set. SimPO consistently achieves higher reward accuracy than DPO, suggesting\n1437-that our reward design facilitates better generalization and leads to higher quality generations.\n1438:KL divergence of SimPO and DPO. In Figure 5a, we present the KL divergence between the\n1439:policy model trained with DPO and SimPO and the reference model with different β, measured\n1440-on the winning responses from a held-out set during training. Figure 5b shows the corresponding\n1441:AlpacaEval 2 LC win rate. Although SimPO does not apply any form of regularization against\n1442:the reference model, the KL divergence of SimPO is reasonably small. Increasing β reduces the\n1443:KL divergence for both DPO and SimPO, with DPO exhibiting a more pronounced reduction at\n1444-higher β values. In this particular setting (Mistral-base), Figure 5b demonstrates that a smaller β can\n1445-10\n1446-Note that this correlation does not fully reflect the generation length. Despite DPO showing a stronger\n1447-correlation, the length of its generated responses is comparable to or even slightly shorter than those of the\n1448:SimPO models. Please find more details in Appendix E.\n1449-\n1450-9\n1451-\n1452-\fimprove AlpacaEval 2 performance, despite the higher KL divergence.11 We hypothesize that when\n1453-the reference model is weak, strictly constraining the policy model to the reference model may not be\n1454-beneficial. As a caveat, while we did not observe any training collapse or degeneration with proper\n1455:tuning, in principle, SimPO could potentially lead to reward hacking without explicit regularization\n1456:against the reference model. In such a scenario, the model might achieve a low loss but degenerate.\n1457:SimPO is more memory and compute-efficient than DPO. Another benefit of SimPO is its\n1458-efficiency as it does not use a reference model. Figure 5c illustrates the overall run time and per-GPU\n1459:peak memory usage of SimPO and DPO in the Llama-3-Base setting using 8×H100 GPUs. Compared\n1460:to a vanilla DPO implementation,12 SimPO cuts run time by roughly 20% and reduces GPU memory\n1461-usage by about 10%, thanks to eliminating forward passes with the reference model.\n1462-\n1463-5\n--\n1482-this approach to an iterative training setup, by continuously updating the reference model with the\n1483-most recent policy model or generating new preference pairs at each iteration [27, 46, 67, 87, 92]. In\n1484-this work, we focus exclusively on offline settings, avoiding any iterative training processes.\n1485:Preference optimization objectives. A variety of preference optimization objectives have been\n1486:proposed besides DPO. Ranking objectives allow for comparisons among more than two instances [26,\n1487:58, 72, 91]. Another line of work explores simpler preference optimization objectives that do not rely\n1488:on a reference model [42, 89], similar to SimPO. [8] proposes a method to jointly optimize instructions\n1489-and responses, finding it effectively improves DPO. [98] focuses on post-training extrapolation\n1490-between the SFT and the aligned model to further enhance model performance. In this work, we\n1491:compare SimPO to a series of offline algorithms, including RRHF [91], SLiC-HF [96], DPO [66], IPO\n1492:[6], CPO [88], KTO [29], ORPO [42], and R-DPO [64], and find that SimPO can outperform them\n1493-in both efficiency and performance. Recently, [75] proposed a generalized preference optimization\n1494:framework unifying different offline algorithms, and SimPO can be seen as a special case.\n1495-\n1496-6\n1497-\n1498-Conclusion\n1499-\n1500:In this work, we propose SimPO, a simple and effective preference optimization algorithm that\n1501-consistently outperforms existing approaches across various training setups. By aligning the reward\n1502:function with the generation likelihood and introducing a target reward margin, SimPO eliminates\n1503-the need for a reference model and achieves strong performance without exploiting the length bias.\n1504:Extensive analysis demonstrates that the key designs in SimPO are crucial and validates the efficiency\n1505:and effectiveness of SimPO. A detailed discussion of the limitations can be found in Appendix A.\n1506-11\n1507-\n1508-We observe that in some settings (e.g., Llama-3-Instruct), a large β (e.g., β = 10) leads to better performance.\n1509:DPO can be as memory efficient as SimPO if it were implemented to separate the forward passes of the\n1510-reference model from the actual preference optimization. However, this implementation is not standard practice.\n1511-12\n1512-\n--\n1763-Advancing open-source language models with mixed-quality data. In ICLR, 2024.\n1764-[83] Haoxiang Wang, Yong Lin, Wei Xiong, Rui Yang, Shizhe Diao, Shuang Qiu, Han Zhao, and\n1765-Tong Zhang. Arithmetic control of LLMs for diverse user preferences: Directional preference\n1766:alignment with multi-objective rewards. ArXiv, abs/2402.18571, 2024.\n1767-[84] Haoxiang Wang, Wei Xiong, Tengyang Xie, Han Zhao, and Tong Zhang. Interpretable\n1768:preferences via multi-objective reward modeling and mixture-of-experts. arXiv preprint\n1769-arXiv:2406.12845, 2024.\n1770-[85] Yizhong Wang, Hamish Ivison, Pradeep Dasigi, Jack Hessel, Tushar Khot, Khyathi Chandu,\n1771-David Wadden, Kelsey MacMillan, Noah A Smith, Iz Beltagy, et al. How far can camels go?\n--\n1780-Kenton Murray, and Young Jin Kim. Contrastive preference optimization: Pushing the\n1781-boundaries of LLM performance in machine translation. ArXiv, abs/2401.08417, 2024.\n1782-[89] Jing Xu, Andrew Lee, Sainbayar Sukhbaatar, and Jason Weston. Some things are more\n1783:cringe than others: Preference optimization with the pairwise cringe loss. arXiv preprint\n1784-arXiv:2312.16682, 2023.\n1785-[90] Shusheng Xu, Wei Fu, Jiaxuan Gao, Wenjie Ye, Weilin Liu, Zhiyu Mei, Guangju Wang, Chao\n1786-Yu, and Yi Wu. Is DPO superior to PPO for LLM alignment? a comprehensive study. arXiv\n--\n1826-Limitations\n1827-\n1828-More in-depth theoretical analysis. Despite the empirical success and intuitive motivation of\n1829:SimPO, a more rigorous theoretical analysis is necessary to fully understand the factors contributing\n1830-to its effectiveness. Additionally, we introduce an additional hyperparameter, the target reward\n1831-margin, which requires manual tuning. Future work could explore how to determine the optimal\n1832:margin automatically and provide a more theoretical understanding of SimPO.\n1833:Safety and honesty. SimPO is designed to optimize the generation quality of language models\n1834-by pushing the margin between the average log likelihood of the winning response and the losing\n1835-response to exceed a target reward margin. However, it does not explicitly consider safety and honesty\n1836-aspects, which are crucial for real-world applications. Future work should explore integrating safety\n1837:and honesty constraints into SimPO to ensure that the generated responses are not only high-quality\n1838-but also safe and honest. The dataset used in this work, UltraFeedback [23], primarily focuses on\n1839-helpfulness, and future research may consider a more comprehensive study utilizing larger-scale\n1840-preference datasets [43, 95] and evaluation benchmarks [81] that place a strong emphasis on safety\n1841-aspects. Nonetheless, we observe that this method consistently achieves high TruthfulQA [57]\n1842:performance compared to other objectives in Table 9, suggesting its potential for safety alignment.\n1843-Performance drop on math. We observed that preference optimization algorithms generally\n1844-decrease downstream task performance, particularly on reasoning-heavy tasks like GSM8k, as shown\n1845:in Table 9. SimPO occasionally results in performance comparable to or worse than DPO. We\n1846-hypothesize that this may be related to the choice of training datasets, hyperparameters used for\n1847-training, or a mismatch of chat templates used for downstream task evaluations. One explanation is\n1848:that the preference optimization objective may not be effectively increasing the likelihood of preferred\n1849-sequences despite increasing the reward margin. [63] first observed this phenomenon and point out\n1850-that this can hinder learning from math preference pairs where changing one token can flip the label\n1851-(e.g., changing 2 + 2 = 4 to 2 + 2 = 5). They propose a simple regularization strategy to add back a\n1852:reference-model calibrated supervised fine-tuning loss to the preference optimization objective, and\n1853-effectively mitigate this issue. Future work may consider integrating this regularization strategy into\n1854:SimPO to improve performance on reasoning-heavy tasks.\n1855-\n1856-B\n1857-\n--\n1862-underestimated in prior research, potentially leading to suboptimal baseline results. To ensure a\n1863-fair comparison, we conduct thorough hyperparameter tuning for all methods compared in our\n1864-experiments.\n1865:General training hyperparameters. For the Table 8: The hyperparameter values in SimPO\n1866-Base training setups, we train SFT models using used for each training setting.\n1867-the UltraChat-200k dataset [25] with the following\n1868-Setting\n--\n1896-Therefore, we individually search the learning rates in the range of [3e-7, 5e-7, 6e-7, 1e-6] for each\n1897-18\n1898-\n1899:\fTable 7: Various preference optimization objectives and hyperparameter search range.\n1900-Method\n1901-RRHF [91]\n1902-\n1903:Objective\n1904-\u0010\n1905-\u0011\n1906-max 0, − |y1w | log πθ (yw |x) + |y1l | log πθ (yl |x) − λ log πθ (yw |x)\n--\n1932-\n1933-IPO [6]\n1934-\n1935:SimPO\n1936-\n1937-− log σ\n1938-\n--\n2055-\n2056-0\n2057-\n2058:(b) Contingency table (SimPO).\n2059-\n2060-Figure 6: (a) Likelihood-length correlation plot for Mistral-SFT fine-tuned on UltraChat-200k. (a)\n2061:Contingency table rankings based on SimPO rewards and the average log likelihood (measured on\n2062-the training set).\n2063-\n2064-method. Table 7 shows the detailed information on method-specific hyperparameters search ranges\n2065:for baselines.13 Table 8 shows SimPO’s hyperparameters used under each setting.\n2066-Decoding hyperparameters. For AlpacaEval 2, we use a sampling decoding strategy to generate\n2067:responses, with a temperature of 0.7 for the Mistral-Base setting following zephyr-7b-beta,14 a\n2068-temperature of 0.5 for the Mistral-Instruct setting following Snorkel-Mistral-PairRM-DPO,\n2069-and a temperature of 0.9 for both Llama 3 settings.15 For Arena-Hard, we use the default greedy\n2070-decoding for all settings and methods. For MT-Bench, we follow the official decoding configuration\n--\n2076-optimizer [68]. We use an Adam optimizer [47] for all the experiments.\n2077-14\n2078-https://github.com/tatsu-lab/alpaca_eval/blob/main/src/alpaca_eval/\n2079:models_configs/zephyr-7b-beta/configs.yaml\n2080-15\n2081-We grid search the temperature hyperparameter for the Llama-3-Base setting with DPO over\n2082-0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, and fix it for all different methods.\n--\n2092-To examine how preference optimization methods affect downstream task performance, we evaluate\n2093-models trained with different methods on various tasks listed on the Huggingface Open Leaderboard [9]. These tasks include MMLU [38], ARC [19], HellaSwag [94], TruthfulQA [57], Winograd [52], and GSM8K [20]. We follow the established evaluation protocols and present the results\n2094-for all models in Table 9. Generally, we find that preference optimization’s effect varies across tasks.\n2095:Knowledge is largely retained with a small loss. Compared to the SFT checkpoint, we find that\n2096-all preference optimization methods generally maintain MMLU performance with minimal decline.\n2097:In this aspect, SimPO is largely comparable to DPO.\n2098-Reading comprehension and commonsense reasoning improves. For ARC and HellaSwag,\n2099-preference optimization methods generally improve performance compared to the SFT checkpoint.\n2100-One hypothesis is that the preference optimization dataset contains similar prompts to these tasks,\n--\n2107-generate more truthful responses.\n2108-Math performance drops. GSM8K is the benchmark that shows the most volatility across methods.\n2109-Notably, except for ORPO, almost all approaches lead to consistent drops in one or more settings.\n2110:We hypothesize that ORPO retains performance largely due to its supervised fine-tuning loss for\n2111:regulation. [63] adds a reference-model calibrated supervised fine-tuning loss to the preference\n2112:optimization objective, and find that it effectively solves the issue and maintains performance on\n2113-math tasks as well.\n2114-Overall, identifying a pattern in downstream performance is challenging. Comprehensive analysis is\n2115:difficult due to using different pretrained models, preference optimization datasets, and objectives.\n2116-Recent works indicate that gradient-based approaches could be effective in finding relevant data\n2117-for downstream tasks [86], and could possibly extended to understand the effect of preference\n2118-optimization. We believe a thorough study on how preference optimization affects downstream\n--\n2130-\n2131-Generation Length Analysis\n2132-\n2133:Length normalization decreases generation length and improves generation quality. Removing length normalization from the SimPO objective results in an approach similar to Contrastive\n2134-Preference Optimization (CPO) [88], which interpolates reward maximization with a supervised\n2135:fine-tuning loss and has demonstrated strong performance in machine translation. However, without\n2136:the supervised fine-tuning loss, the reward maximization objective without length normalization is\n2137-suboptimal in preference optimization.\n2138-We analyze the generation length of models trained with or without length normalization on AlpacaEval 2 and Arena-Hard. As shown in Figure 6, length normalization significantly decrease the\n2139-generation length by up to 25% compared to when it is not used in most cases. However, even though\n--\n2332-KTO\n2333-ORPO\n2334-R-DPO\n2335:SimPO\n2336-\n2337-40.35\n2338-43.73\n--\n2355-KTO\n2356-ORPO\n2357-R-DPO\n2358:SimPO\n2359-\n2360-60.40\n2361-59.75\n--\n2411-KTO\n2412-ORPO\n2413-R-DPO\n2414:SimPO\n2415-\n2416-64.88\n2417-64.71\n--\n2455-KTO\n2456-ORPO\n2457-R-DPO\n2458:SimPO\n2459-\n2460-67.06\n2461-67.20\n--\n2519-all settings. Additionally, even though some methods may generate longer responses, they do not\n2520-necessarily achieve better win rates on the benchmarks. This indicates that the length of the generated\n2521-responses is not a reliable indicator of generation quality.\n2522:SimPO demonstrates minimal exploitation of response length. We observe that SimPO has\n2523-a shorter generation length compared to DPO in the Llama-3-Instruct case but exhibits a higher\n2524-generation length in other settings, with up to 26% longer responses on AlpacaEval 2. Conversely,\n2525:SimPO only increases length by only around 5% on Arena-Hard compared to DPO. It is fair to say\n2526-that the generation length heavily depends on the evaluation benchmark. A stronger indicator is that\n2527:SimPO consistently achieves a higher length-controlled win rate on AlpacaEval 2 compared to the\n2528-raw win rate, demonstrating minimal exploitation of response length.\n2529-21\n2530-\n--\n2587-\n2588-A >= B\n2589-\n2590:A = SimPO\n2591-\n2592-%\n2593-\n2594:A = SimPO\n2595-\n2596-(a) Mistral-Base.\n2597-\n--\n2753-Figure 7: Win rate heatmap of Mistral-Base and Mistral-Instruct on AlpacaEval 2. B represents the\n2754-baseline model (i.e., GPT-4-Preview-1106).\n2755-\n2756:A = SimPO\n2757-\n2758-15\n2759-\n--\n2763-\n2764-0\n2765-\n2766:A = SimPO\n2767-\n2768-(a) Mistral-Base.\n2769-\n--\n2776-\n2777-Gradient Analysis\n2778-\n2779:We examine the gradients of SimPO and DPO to understand their different impact on the training\n2780-process.\n2781-\n2782-\n--\n2793-\n2794-∇θ log πθ (yw |x) −\n2795-∇θ log πθ (yl |x) ,\n2796:∇θ LSimPO (πθ ) = −βE(x,yw ,yl )∼D sθ · \n2797-|yl |\n2798-\n2799- |yw |\n--\n2849-πref (yl |x)\n2850-πref (yw |x)\n2851-\n2852:represent the gradient weight in SimPO and DPO, respectively. It can be seen that the differences\n2853:are twofold: (1) comparing the gradient weights sθ and dθ , SimPO’s gradient weight sθ does not\n2854-involve the reference model and has a straightforward interpretation: the weights will be higher for\n2855-samples where the policy model incorrectly assigns higher likelihood to yl than yw ; (2) comparing\n2856:the gradient updates, SimPO’s gradients on yl and yw are length-normalized, while DPO’s are not.\n2857-This corresponds to the empirical findings [64] that DPO may exploit length bias: longer sequences\n2858-with more tokens will receive larger gradient updates in DPO, dominating the training process.\n2859-22\n--\n2863-Qualitative Analysis\n2864-\n2865-We present the win rate heatmap of Mistral-Base and Mistral-Instruct on AlpacaEval 2 and ArenaHard in Figure 7 and Figure 8, respectively. Based on this analysis, we present qualitative examples of\n2866:responses generated by a SimPO model, a DPO model and the baseline model GPT-4-Preview-1106\n2867-on AlpacaEval 2.\n2868:Comparing SimPO with DPO. In Figure 9 and Figure 10, we present an example where MistralBase-SimPO generates a better-structured answer compared to Mistral-Base-DPO. Given the question,\n2869-\"How can you determine if a person is genuinely interested in a conversation or simply being polite?\",\n2870-the DPO model generates a response with a long list of bullet points, making it difficult to understand\n2871:the relationships between different points. In contrast, the SimPO model produces a well-structured\n2872-answer with high-level categorization of different behaviors, followed by detailed suggestions for\n2873-each category. This makes the answer more readable and easier to understand.\n2874:Comparing Instruct models with Base models when trained with SimPO. In Figure 11, we\n2875-present an example where Llama-3-Instruct generates a more detailed and well-formatted answer\n2876:compared to the baseline model, and as well as the Llama-3-Base-SimPO model. Given the question:\n2877:What language does Argentina people speak? Llama-3-Base-SimPO only gives a very brief answer.\n2878-GPT-4-Preview-1106 gives a more detailed answer in explaining how the Argentina Spanish differs\n2879:from standard Spanish. However, the answer is not well formatted and a bit hard to parse. Llama-3Instruct-SimPO gives a detailed and well-formatted answer, which is easier to read and understand,\n2880-and offers sufficient details.\n2881-\n2882-23\n--\n2958-KTO\n2959-ORPO\n2960-R-DPO\n2961:SimPO\n2962-\n2963-8.4\n2964-11.6\n--\n3190-KTO\n3191-ORPO\n3192-R-DPO\n3193:SimPO\n3194-\n3195-17.1\n3196-25.3\n--\n3246-KTO\n3247-ORPO\n3248-R-DPO\n3249:SimPO\n3250-\n3251-6.2\n3252-10.8\n--\n3290-KTO\n3291-ORPO\n3292-R-DPO\n3293:SimPO\n3294-\n3295-26.0\n3296-31.3\n--\n3366-AlpacaEval 2\n3367-Model\n3368-\n3369:SimPO\n3370:SimPO w/o LN\n3371-\n3372-Mistral-Base\n3373-\n--\n3421-\n3422-26\n3423-\n3424:\fFigure 10: An AlpacaEval 2 generation from the Mistral-Base model after training with SimPO.\n3425:Compared to the output generated by the DPO model, as shown in Figure 9, the generation