 for SimPO under the Llama-3 Instruct v0.2 setting: β = 10\n3907-and γ = 3. The other hyperparameters (e.g., learning rate, batch size, max sequence lengths) are kept\n3908-the same as the original Llama-3-8B-Instruct setting.\n3909-29\n--\n3911-\fStrong SFT model and high-quality policy data diminish algorithm differences. With a strong\n3912-SFT model like Llama-3-8B-Instruct, and as the preference optimization data quality improves, the\n3913-differences between algorithms become less pronounced. For instance, DPO achieved a similar win\n3914:rate as SimPO in terms of raw win rate, and DPO, IPO, and R-DPO all exhibited comparable raw\n3915:win rates on Arena-Hard. However, SimPO maintains an advantage by producing shorter sequences,\n3916-resulting in a significantly better LC win rate on AlpacaEval 2.\n3917-Stronger downstream task performance. The v0.2 ver- Table 14: AlpacaEval 2 performance of\n3918:sion also shows improved performance in downstream tasks SimPO and SimPO with an SFT loss.\n3919:across various objectives. However, DPO, IPO, R-DPO,\n3920:and SimPO continue to experience a decline in reasoningMethod\n3921-LC (%) WR (%)\n3922:intensive domains such as GSM8K. In contrast, objectives\n3923:SimPO v0.2\n3924-53.7\n3925-47.5\n3926-that include an SFT component maintain their performance\n--\n3928-41.4\n3929-36.5\n3930-in mathematical tasks.\n3931:Incorporating SFT regularization in SimPO. Several reference-free algorithms, including\n3932:RRHF [91], SLiC-HF [96], CPO [88], and ORPO [42], employ SFT regularization in their objectives. SFT regularization can be an effective method to prevent reward hacking, ensuring that the\n3933:solution maintains low loss without resulting in degraded generations. We also experiment with the\n3934:integration of an SFT loss in SimPO, yielding the following objective:\n3935-\u0013\n3936-\u0015\n3937-\u0014\n--\n3940-β\n3941-log πθ (yw |x) −\n3942-log πθ (yl |x) − γ + λ log πθ (yw |x) .\n3943:LSimPO w/ SFT (πθ ) = −E(x,yw ,yl )∼D log σ\n3944-|yw |\n3945-|yl |\n3946-\n--\n3956-2024)\n3957-\n3958-Since the release of the paper, we have had inquiries from researchers about whether the key design\n3959:elements of SimPO—length normalization and target reward margin—could benefit DPO. By doing\n3960:so, we will derive the following two objectives:\n3961-\u0014\n3962-\u0012\n3963-\u0013\u0015\n--\n3987-reward design being implicitly normalized by the reference model, the policy model might still exploit\n3988-length bias from the data, resulting in a disproportionately high probability for longer sequences.\n3989-Applying length normalization could help mitigate this effect.\n3990:We train models with the objectives mentioned above and compare their performance to that of DPO\n3991:and SimPO, as shown in Table 15.\n3992:The results indicate that, unlike SimPO, length normalization and target reward margin do not\n3993-consistently benefit DPO. Specifically, length normalization significantly improves DPO performance\n3994-only in the Mistral-Base setting, where the preference optimization dataset shows a strong length\n3995-bias. However, it does not provide a benefit in the Mistral-Instruct setting, where the lengths of\n--\n4028-\n4029-WR (%)\n4030-\n4031:SimPO\n4032-\n4033-21.5\n4034-\n--\n4106-\n4107-GPT-4 Turbo GPT-4\n4108-\n4109:Applying SimPO to Gemma 2 Models (Sept 16, 2024)\n4110-\n4111:Performance degradation on other benchmarks for Llama-3-SimPO checkpoints. After releasing the Llama-3-SimPO checkpoints, we received extensive feedback about performance degradation\n4112-on benchmarks measuring specific capabilities, such as MMLU and GSM8K. To investigate this\n4113-issue, we continued training the Llama-3-8B-Instruct model with different learning rates, as reported\n4114-in Table 16. We find that using a higher learning rate results in a stronger model in chat-oriented\n--\n4120-from Llama-3-8B-Instruct with different learning rates. * indicates the released checkpoint.\n4121-Model\n4122-Llama-3-8B-Instruct\n4123:SimPO (lr=4e-7)\n4124:SimPO (lr=5e-7)\n4125:SimPO (lr=1e-6)*\n4126-\n4127-AlpacaEval 2\n4128-LC (%)\n--\n4148-62.3\n4149-54.9\n4150-\n4151:Applying SimPO to Gemma 2 models presents a different trend. We evaluate SimPO using\n4152-Google’s recently released Gemma-2-9B-it model [77], which represents a strong open-source model.\n4153-For training data, we generate up to 5 responses per prompt from the UltraFeedback dataset [23] and\n4154:use the ArmoRM model [84] to annotate preferences between responses. We compare our SimPO\n4155-against a DPO-trained variant, both fine-tuned from the Gemma-2-9B-it base model. As shown in\n4156:Appendix J, SimPO demonstrates superior performance on chat benchmarks like AlpacaEval 2 and\n4157-Arena-Hard while maintaining the model’s original zero-shot capabilities on tasks like GSM8K and\n4158-MMLU. Notably, we find that varying the learning rate during fine-tuning has minimal impact on the\n4159-model’s performance. These results suggest an underlying property difference between the Llama-3\n4160-checkpoints and the Gemma 2 checkpoints, and might be worth further investigation.\n4161:Gemma-2-9B-it-SimPO significantly improved the ranking of the Gemma-2-9B-it model on\n4162-Chatbot Arena. During the development stage, we relied solely on automated metrics to evaluate\n4163-the model’s performance. To determine if these metrics aligned with real user preferences, we\n4164:submitted our best-performing model, Gemma-2-9B-it-SimPO, to the Chatbot Arena leaderboard\n4165-hosted by LMSYS [17]. We find that our model improved the original Gemma-2-9B-it ranking\n4166:from 36th to 25th, making the SimPO variant the top-ranked <10B model on the Chatbot Arena\n4167-leaderboard based on real user votes as of September 16th, 2024.\n4168-\n4169-17\n--\n4173-\n4174-31\n4175-\n4176:\fTable 17: Benchmark performance of Gemma-2-9B trained with DPO and SimPO on UltraFeedback\n4177-(responses regenerated with Gemma-2-9B-it, following the same dataset construction process as\n4178:Llama-3-Instruct (8B) described in Section 3). SimPO results in better instruction following performance than DPO without degrading math abilities (GSM) or general knowledge (MMLU) of the\n4179-original model. * indicates the released checkpoint.\n4180-Model\n4181-\n--\n4190-GSM (%) MMLU (%)\n4191-Gemma-2-9B-it\n4192-Gemma-2-9B-DPO\n4193:Gemma-2-9B-SimPO (lr=6e-7)\n4194:Gemma-2-9B-SimPO (lr=8e-7)*\n4195:Gemma-2-9B-SimPO (lr=1e-6)\n4196-\n4197-51.1\n4198-67.8","numLines":1035},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ca1169e3-43e5-4f35-a72f-f53f05e9daf6"}
